Who said science experiments with kitchen appliances are cheesy? This one explains why your microwave oven has that spinning tray.

@alex.dainis explains the science behind microwaves and shows you how you can use cheese to see the length of a wave!  🧀

#Microwaves #ScienceExperiment #WaveLength

Is this candle haunted? 🕯️👻

@alexdainis places two candles on a lazy Susan with a piece of glass in between, and when the closer candle is lit, its flame appears to jump to the other one. Most light passes through the glass, but some of the light from the close candle bounces back at us, creating a reflection that makes the flame seem to appear on the far candle. This optical illusion is similar to Pepper's Ghost!

#Illusion #Light #Science #STEM #ScienceDemo

How do you find out you’ve won the Nobel Prize?

@NobelPrize winner Eric Cornell breaks down the process. You can't apply, someone else has to nominate you, and nominees aren’t told they’ve been nominated. Deliberations happen in secret, so the news is as much a surprise for the winner as for everyone else. Announcements come at noon in Stockholm, which is 4 a.m. in Boulder, Colorado, so when Eric’s phone rang at 4:30am on Nobel Prize morning, he woke up right away!

#NobelPrize #Science #Chemistry #STEM #EricCornell

Did you know slime can be a helpful cleaning tool? 🫟

@wolf.science tweaks the classic glue and borax recipe to make slime tacky enough to grab dirt but clean enough to pull away. The chemistry: borate ions cross-link glue's long polymer chains, turning runny glue into slime. Less activator makes it stretchy like the toy, while just enough makes it a surprisingly satisfying debris grabber.

#ScienceExperiments #Science #Chemistry #Slime #DIY

